# Invention No. 7 in E minor, BWV 778 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a sighing, lamenting piece. The subject (after an eighth
# rest) is a sixteenth turn on the E minor chord and two ornamented
# eighths; the bass punctuates each bar with a falling octave and then
# echoes the subject in the bar's second half, so the two hands answer
# each other every half bar. Long trilled notes in the middle and a
# long B pedal near the end. It wants a gentle, singing tempo.
#
# Time signature: 4/4, 23 bars in the Mutopia score and in the MIDI;
# the bass E is on the downbeat of bar 1 (the top enters after an
# eighth rest).
#
# Tempo: quarter = 66, 92 quarters, about 1:25.
# Track times: Gustav Leonhardt (harpsichord, Apple Music reissue)
# 1:31, about 61; Angela Hewitt (Hyperion) 1:07, about 82; Andras
# Schiff (ECM, clavichord) 1:41, about 55; Glenn Gould (Sony, 1964)
# 0:55, about 100, a quick outlier. 66 sits between Leonhardt and
# Hewitt: unhurried and singing, but the line keeps moving on the chip.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the key plan is my own reading):
#   1-4    Subject, top, each bar, echoed by the bass in each second
#          half bar; E minor, B (2), and onward.
#   5-6    Sequence in sixteenths, both hands.
#   7-9    Cadence in G major (7); a long trilled D held in the top
#          (7-8) while the bass runs; cadence in D major (9).
#   10-13  Subject material in both hands, through E minor to B minor;
#          cadence in B minor at bar 13 (beat 3).
#   14-17  A long B held in the bass (15-17), the dominant pedal, while
#          the top runs a falling sequence.
#   18-19  The falling figures pass to the bass, top broken chords.
#   20-21  The subject's figures return in E minor.
#   22-23  Final cadence in E minor with a fermata, no Picardy third.

tempo 66

voice mid 0          # two voices only: there is no middle voice to set back

# No lead marks in bars 1-4: the subject passes between the hands every
# half bar, and a lead mark lasts at least a bar, so it would lift the
# wrong voice half the time. Even balance is right there.

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-6: the exposition in E minor
dyn 7 mp             # bars 7-9: G and D major, the long trilled D: a
                     # softer, lighter manual
lead 7-8 bottom      # the bass runs under the held D
dyn 10 mf            # bars 10-13: toward B minor
broaden 12 0.94      # bar 12: hold back into the B minor cadence

dyn 14 mp            # bars 14-19: the B pedal, starting quietly
ramp 15-19 f         # the one long build, over the pedal and the
                     # falling bass, into the return
                     # (bars 20-23 stay at f to the end)

rit 2 0.7            # bars 22-23 into the final E minor
